\title{Almost-Lossless Compression Beyond the Pigeonhole Bound:\\
Exact Converses, Certified Decoders, and Logarithmic Decoding Cost}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The pigeonhole principle governs \emph{exact} decoding: a compression scheme
recovering every symbol of a finite source alphabet $\alpha$ requires at least
$\abs{\alpha}$ codewords. We develop the \emph{almost-lossless} relaxation, in
which the decoder may abstain or err with total probability at most
$\varepsilon$, and determine the exact price of that relaxation in three
currencies at once: rate, reliability, and decoding time.

On the rate side we prove a localized pigeonhole bound---the encoder is injective
on the set of correctly decoded symbols---whence
$\Pr[\text{success}] \le \abs{\Code}\pmaxx$ and
$\log\abs{\Code} \ge \Hinf(\mu) + \log(1-\varepsilon)$; for every code size
$M \le n$ we exhibit a scheme on the uniform source over $n$ symbols with success
probability exactly $M/n$, so the relaxed bound is attained.

On the achievability side we replace Shannon's unbounded random codebook by a
$2$-universal hash family keyed by a single element and instrument the decoder so
that its cost is proved rather than estimated. A first-moment argument yields an
explicit key with failure probability at most $\delta + \abs{l}/M$ on a codebook
$l$ whose complement carries mass $\delta$; a two-region threshold argument
sharpens silent corruption to $2\delta\abs{l}/M$, and a one-parameter version
gives $(1+\eta)\delta\abs{l}/M$ against failure $\delta+(1+\eta^{-1})\abs{l}/M$
for every $\eta>0$. Pairing a checksum family multiplies the collision parameter,
driving silent corruption to $\abs{l}/(MC)$ at $\log C$ extra bits. Non-vacuity
is supplied by the inner-product family $h_k(x_1,x_2)=x_1+kx_2$ over
$\mathbb{F}_p$, compressing $p^2$ symbols to $p$ codewords.

The principal contribution is algorithmic. Sorting the codebook by hash value
\emph{after} the key is chosen---a permutation, hence invisible to the collision
analysis---turns the unique-match scan into a binary search. We give a
cost-instrumented binary search with the exact bound $\log_2 n + 1$ key
evaluations, add a two-neighbour uniqueness test forcing abstention on duplicate
hash values, and prove the resulting decoder never corrupts silently \emph{under
any hypothesis on the hash}. Total cost: at most $\log_2 n + 3$. A decision-tree
converse shows every adaptive Boolean-query decoder correct on $n$ symbols has
worst-case cost at least $\log_2 n$, so the decoder is optimal within an additive
$3$. Finally, list-$T$ decoding relaxes the counting bound by exactly $\log T$
and improves failure linearly under $2$-universality and exponentially, to
$\delta + (\abs{l}/M)^T$, under $(T{+}1)$-wise independence realized by degree-$T$
polynomials with $p^{T+1}$ keys; and integrality of a collision count forces
$K \ge M$ for any compressing $2$-universal family, attained at $K=M=p$.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} almost-lossless compression, universal hashing,
min-entropy, random coding, list decoding, decision-tree lower bounds,
derandomization.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The exact bound and its rigidity}

Let $\alpha$ be a finite source alphabet and $\Code$ a finite code space. A
\emph{compression scheme} is a pair
\[
\enc : \alpha \to \Code, \qquad \dec : \Code \to \alpha \cup \{\bot\},
\]
where $\bot$ denotes an explicit refusal to answer. Demanding
$\dec(\enc(x)) = x$ for all $x$ makes $\enc$ injective, so
$\abs{\alpha} \le \abs{\Code}$: no compression is possible. This pigeonhole bound
is insensitive to computational resources, to source structure, and to the
designer's ingenuity.

Almost-lossless compression relaxes the universal quantifier. Fix a distribution
$\mu$ on $\alpha$ and require only
$\Pr_{x\sim\mu}[\dec(\enc(x)) = x] \ge 1-\varepsilon$. The folklore is that random
codebooks then achieve near-optimal rates. What the folklore does not supply is
(i) an exact statement of how far the counting bound moves, (ii) a guarantee that
failures are never silent, and (iii) an implementable decoder with a proved
running time. Random coding as usually presented is a proof technique: its
decoder is an exhaustive search over an astronomically large codebook.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Exact rate relaxation} (\S\ref{sec:converse}): the pigeonhole bound
survives in localized form and yields
$\log\abs{\Code} \ge \Hinf(\mu) + \log(1-\varepsilon)$, which is attained.
\item \textbf{Certified achievability} (\S\ref{sec:rc}--\S\ref{sec:silence}): a
$2$-universal family with a unique-match decoder gives failure
$\delta+\abs{l}/M$, never lies on a codebook symbol, and admits sharpened,
tunable, and checksum-amplified silent-error bounds.
\item \textbf{Logarithmic decoding} (\S\ref{sec:cost}--\S\ref{sec:log}): sorting
the codebook after key selection yields a binary-search decoder of proved cost
$\le \log_2 n + 3$ that never corrupts silently under any hypothesis, matched by a
decision-tree converse of $\log_2 n$.
\item \textbf{List decoding} (\S\ref{sec:list}): exact $\log T$ rate relaxation;
linear gain from $2$-universality, exponential gain from $(T{+}1)$-wise
independence with short polynomial keys.
\item \textbf{The cost of randomness} (\S\ref{sec:keys}): the sharp bound
$K \ge M$, attained.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Cost model}
Throughout, \emph{cost} means the number of key evaluations---applications of the
hash to a candidate symbol---performed on one query. Every cost statement is an
exact arithmetic bound on an instrumented algorithm, never an asymptotic
estimate. The lower bounds of \S\ref{sec:log} are stated in the same currency for
a strictly more general model, so the two sides are directly comparable.

\section{Schemes, success, and silence}

\begin{definition}[Scheme]
A \emph{scheme} on a finite alphabet $\alpha$ with code space $\Code$ is a pair
$(\enc,\dec)$ with $\enc:\alpha\to\Code$ and $\dec:\Code\to\alpha\cup\{\bot\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The scheme \emph{succeeds} at $x$ if $\dec(\enc(x)) = x$; it \emph{silently
corrupts} $x$ if $\dec(\enc(x)) = y$ for some $y \ne x$ with $y \ne \bot$; it is
\emph{never silent} if it silently corrupts no symbol.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:neversilent}
A scheme is never silent if and only if $\dec(\enc(x)) \in \{x,\bot\}$ for all $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate: a confident answer that is not $x$ is a silent corruption, and
conversely.
\end{proof}

The distinction is operational. A scheme that abstains composes with
retransmission, fallback, or an outer error-correcting layer; a scheme that lies
confidently cannot be repaired by any outer layer, because the outer layer
receives no signal.

\begin{definition}[Success set, min-entropy]
The \emph{success set} is $\Succ = \{x : \dec(\enc(x)) = x\}$; the \emph{success
probability} is $\mu(\Succ)$. We write $\pmaxx(\mu) = \max_x \mu(x)$ and
$\Hinf(\mu) = -\log \pmaxx(\mu)$.
\end{definition}

Min-entropy, not Shannon entropy, is the correct parameter: the arguments are
counting arguments, and a set of $m$ symbols carries at most $m\pmaxx$ mass.

\section{The relaxed pigeonhole bound and its tightness}\label{sec:converse}

\begin{theorem}[Localized pigeonhole]\label{thm:local}
For every scheme, $\enc$ is injective on $\Succ$; consequently
$\abs{\Succ} \le \abs{\Code}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x,y\in\Succ$ and $\enc(x)=\enc(y)$ then
$x = \dec(\enc(x)) = \dec(\enc(y)) = y$.
\end{proof}

Failure and abstention buy nothing combinatorially: however wildly the decoder
behaves off $\Succ$, the part where it behaves must fit inside the code space.

\begin{corollary}[Exact pigeonhole]
If the scheme succeeds at every symbol then $\abs{\alpha} \le \abs{\Code}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Counting bound]\label{thm:count}
For every scheme, $\Pr[\text{success}] \le \abs{\Code}\cdot\pmaxx(\mu)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mu(\Succ) = \sum_{x\in\Succ}\mu(x) \le \abs{\Succ}\pmaxx \le
\abs{\Code}\pmaxx$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:local}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Almost-lossless converse]\label{thm:conv}
If $\Pr[\text{success}] \ge 1-\varepsilon$ then
$\abs{\Code} \ge (1-\varepsilon)/\pmaxx(\mu)$, and for $\varepsilon<1$,
\[
\log\abs{\Code} \;\ge\; \Hinf(\mu) + \log(1-\varepsilon).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rearrange Theorem~\ref{thm:count} and take logarithms, using
$\log(1/\pmaxx) = \Hinf$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform source]
For $\mu$ uniform on $n$ symbols, success probability $1-\varepsilon$ forces
$\abs{\Code} \ge (1-\varepsilon)n$.
\end{corollary}

On a flat source the bound degrades by the factor $1-\varepsilon$ and nothing
more: tolerating $1\%$ failure saves about $0.0145$ bits.

\begin{theorem}[Tightness]\label{thm:tight}
For all $n$ and all $M$ with $0 < M \le n$ there is a scheme from the uniform
source on $n$ symbols into a code space of size $M$ whose success probability is
exactly $M/n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Identify the source with $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and the code space with
$\{0,\dots,M-1\}$; encode $x\mapsto x$ if $x<M$ and $x\mapsto 0$ otherwise, and
decode $i\mapsto i$. The success set is $\{0,\dots,M-1\}$, of mass $M/n$.
\end{proof}

Combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:count}, which gives $\le M/n$ here, no converse
above can be strengthened.

\section{Random coding via universal hashing}\label{sec:rc}

\begin{definition}[$2$-universality]
A family $H:\Keys\times\alpha\to[M]$ with $\abs{\Keys}=K$ is \emph{$2$-universal}
if for all $x \ne y$,
\[
\abs{\{k\in\Keys : H_k(x)=H_k(y)\}}\cdot M \;\le\; K .
\]
\end{definition}

The single key $k$ is the entire source of randomness: the concrete substitute
for Shannon's random codebook.

\begin{definition}[Codebook]
A \emph{codebook} is a duplicate-free list $l$ of source symbols with
\emph{typicality parameter} $\delta$, meaning $\mu(\alpha\setminus l)\le\delta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Unique-match decoder]
The encoder sends $H_k(x)$. On receiving $i$, the decoder scans $l$ and returns
the unique $y\in l$ with $H_k(y)=i$ if exactly one exists, and $\bot$ otherwise.
The scan performs exactly $\abs{l}$ key evaluations.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[No silent corruption on the codebook]\label{lem:nosilent}
If $x\in l$ then the decoder returns $x$ or $\bot$, for every key.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$x$ is itself a match for $i=H_k(x)$. Either it is the only one, and the decoder
returns $x$; or there is another and the uniqueness test fails.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Collision set]
$\Coll_k(x) = \{y\in l : y\ne x,\ H_k(y)=H_k(x)\}$. The decoder fails at $x\in l$
precisely when $\Coll_k(x)\ne\emptyset$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Averaging identity]\label{lem:avg}
For $2$-universal $H$ and any region $A\subseteq\alpha$,
\[
M \sum_{k\in\Keys}\mu\bigl(\{x\in A : \Coll_k(x)\ne\emptyset\}\bigr)
\;\le\; K\,\abs{l}\,\mu(A).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Exchange summation. For fixed $x$ and $y\in l$, $y\ne x$, at most $K/M$ keys
satisfy $H_k(y)=H_k(x)$. Sum over the at most $\abs{l}$ choices of $y$ and weight
by $\mu(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Derandomization]\label{thm:derand}
There is a key $k$ whose collision mass on $A$ is at most its average
$\abs{l}\mu(A)/M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A finite family of nonnegative reals contains an element at most the mean; apply
to Lemma~\ref{lem:avg}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Achievability]\label{thm:ach}
Let $H$ be $2$-universal with $K\ge 1$, $M\ge 1$, and let $l$ be a codebook with
typicality parameter $\delta$. There is a key $k$ with
\[
\Pr[\text{failure}] \le \delta + \frac{\abs{l}}{M},\qquad
\Pr[\text{silent corruption}] \le \frac{\abs{l}}{M},
\]
and decoding cost exactly $\abs{l}$ key evaluations per query.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:derand} with $A=\alpha$. Failure at $x$ requires $x\notin
l$ (mass $\le\delta$) or $x\in l$ with a collision (mass $\le\abs{l}/M$). Silent
corruption requires $x\notin l$ \emph{and} a collision, so its mass is at most the
collision mass. The cost is the instrumentation of the scan.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rate sandwich]
If $\pmaxx(\mu)\abs{l}\le c$ and $\abs{l}/M \le \varepsilon-\delta$, then some key
achieves success probability $\ge 1-\varepsilon$ with $M$ codewords, while every
scheme with success probability $\ge 1-\varepsilon$ needs at least
$(1-\varepsilon)\abs{l}/c$ codewords.
\end{theorem}

The gap depends only on $\varepsilon,\delta$ and the flatness constant $c$---not
on $\abs{\alpha}$.

\section{An explicit family}

\begin{definition}[Inner-product family]\label{def:linhash}
For $p$ prime, on the source $\mathbb{F}_p^2$ with key and code spaces
$\mathbb{F}_p$, set $h_k(x_1,x_2) = x_1 + k x_2 \pmod p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
The inner-product family is $2$-universal and compresses $p^2$ symbols into $p$
codewords.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $(x_1,x_2)\ne(y_1,y_2)$ collide then $k(x_2-y_2)=y_1-x_1$. If $x_2\ne y_2$
this determines $k$ uniquely, so $1\cdot p\le p$; if $x_2=y_2$ then $x_1\ne y_1$
and no key collides.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Numeric instance]
Take $p=101$ and a duplicate-free codebook of $10$ symbols carrying all but
$1/100$ of the mass. There is a key $k\in\mathbb{F}_{101}$ with failure
probability at most $1/100 + 10/101 \approx 0.109$, silent corruption at most
$10/101$, and decoding cost exactly $10$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Making silence rarer}\label{sec:silence}

A silent error needs $x\notin l$ \emph{and} $\Coll_k(x)\ne\emptyset$; the first
event has probability $\le\delta$, so the first moment of the silent-error mass
carries an extra factor $\delta$. The obstruction is choosing one key good for two
regions at once.

\begin{lemma}[Markov counting]\label{lem:markov}
For any region $A$ and threshold $c>0$, strictly fewer than $K/c$ keys have
collision mass on $A$ exceeding $c$ times its average $\abs{l}\mu(A)/M$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp silent-error bound]\label{thm:sharp}
There is a single explicit key with
\[
\Pr[\text{failure}] \le \delta + \frac{2\abs{l}}{M}, \qquad
\Pr[\text{silent corruption}] \le \frac{2\delta\abs{l}}{M},
\]
and decoding cost still exactly $\abs{l}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:markov} at threshold $2$ the bad key sets for the region
$\alpha\setminus l$ and for the whole space each have size $<K/2$, so they cannot
cover $\Keys$; a key outside both gives both bounds.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tunable derandomization]\label{thm:tunable}
For every $\eta>0$ there is a key with
\[
\Pr[\text{silent corruption}] \le (1+\eta)\frac{\delta\abs{l}}{M}, \qquad
\Pr[\text{failure}] \le \delta + \Bigl(1+\frac1\eta\Bigr)\frac{\abs{l}}{M},
\]
and decoding cost exactly $\abs{l}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $c_1 = 1+\eta$ and $c_2 = 1+\eta^{-1}$, so $c_1^{-1}+c_2^{-1}=1$ and
Lemma~\ref{lem:markov} leaves a key outside both bad sets.
\end{proof}

Setting $\eta=1$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}; letting $\eta\to 0$ drives the
silent constant to $1$, the first-moment optimum.

\begin{definition}[Paired family]
For $H:\Keys\times\alpha\to[M]$ and $G:\Keys'\times\alpha\to[C]$, set
$(H\otimes G)_{(k,k')}(x) = (H_k(x),G_{k'}(x))$ on key space $\Keys\times\Keys'$
and code space $[M]\times[C]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universality is multiplicative]
If $H$ and $G$ are $2$-universal then so is $H\otimes G$, with $KK'$ keys and
$MC$ codewords.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Two symbols collide under the pair only if they collide in both coordinates;
count key pairs and multiply the two universality bounds.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Guaranteed detection]
Appending a $\log_2 C$-bit checksum from an independent universal family drives
silent corruption to at most $\abs{l}/(MC)$; any target $\eta>0$ is met by
$C > \abs{l}/(M\eta)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Decoding cost, and how to destroy it}\label{sec:cost}

The decoder of \S\ref{sec:rc} costs exactly $\abs{l}$ key evaluations. On a
$b$-fold i.i.d.\ product source the natural codebook is the $b$-fold product
$T^b$ of a per-block typical set, with $\abs{T}^b$ entries: decoding is
exponential in the block length. This, not the rate, is the real obstruction.

\begin{theorem}[Coordinatewise decoding]
Decode each of the $b$ blocks independently against the size-$\abs{T}$ codebook
and assemble the answers. The block decoder returns a full block exactly when
every coordinate decoder does, hence never corrupts silently on the product
codebook; its failure probability is at most $b(\delta + \abs{T}/m)$ and its cost
is exactly $b\abs{T}$ key evaluations.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Exponential speedup]
For $\abs{T}\ge 2$ and $b\ge 3$, $b\abs{T} < \abs{T}^b$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection*{Sorting the codebook}

One might expect a logarithmic decoder to require a family that is
simultaneously $2$-universal and \emph{monotone} on every codebook---an unnatural
and probably unachievable requirement. It is unnecessary.

\begin{remark}[Key observation]
The encoder chooses the key $k$ first, and only then stores the codebook
\emph{sorted by hash value}. Sorting is a permutation of the codebook, and the
collision analysis depends only on the multiset $\{H_k(y) : y \in l\}$, which a
permutation does not change. Every probabilistic bound therefore survives
verbatim, while the stored array now has monotone keys---and a unique-match query
on a monotone array is a binary search.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Sorted codebook]
Let $\sigma$ be a permutation with $H_k(l_{\sigma(0)}) \le H_k(l_{\sigma(1)}) \le
\cdots$; write $\mathrm{key}(i) = H_k(l_{\sigma(i)})$ and $a(i) = l_{\sigma(i)}$.
Then $\mathrm{key}$ is monotone non-decreasing on $\{0,\dots,\abs{l}-1\}$.
\end{definition}

\section{A logarithmic decoder and a matching converse}\label{sec:log}

\begin{definition}[Cost-instrumented binary search]
For $\mathrm{key}:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$, target $t$ and range
$[lo,lo+\mathrm{len})$: if $\mathrm{len}=0$ return $(\bot,0)$; otherwise let
$h=\lfloor \mathrm{len}/2\rfloor$, $m = lo+h$, and
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
\item if $\mathrm{key}(m)=t$, return $(m,1)$;
\item if $\mathrm{key}(m)<t$, recurse on $[m+1,\,m+1+(\mathrm{len}-h-1))$, cost $+1$;
\item otherwise recurse on $[lo,lo+h)$, cost $+1$.
\end{itemize}
The second component counts key evaluations exactly.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact complexity]\label{thm:bscost}
For all $lo$, $\mathrm{len}$:
$\mathrm{cost}(\bsearch(\mathrm{key},t,lo,\mathrm{len})) \le \log_2\mathrm{len}+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $\mathrm{len}$. For $\mathrm{len}\le 1$ the search stops after
at most one evaluation. For $\mathrm{len}\ge 2$ both recursive ranges have size at
most $\lfloor\mathrm{len}/2\rfloor$, and
$\log_2\lfloor\mathrm{len}/2\rfloor + 1 = \log_2\mathrm{len}$ in integer
logarithms; the single midpoint evaluation closes the induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness]\label{thm:bssound}
If the search returns $m$ then $\mathrm{key}(m)=t$ and $lo \le m < lo+\mathrm{len}$.
No monotonicity hypothesis is required.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness]\label{thm:bsfound}
If $\mathrm{key}$ is monotone on $[lo,lo+\mathrm{len})$ and some $j$ there has
$\mathrm{key}(j)=t$, the search returns some index.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction: if the midpoint value is $<t$, monotonicity forces $j>m$, so $j$ lies
in the right subrange, and symmetrically on the other side.
\end{proof}

The split is load-bearing: soundness holds for an \emph{arbitrary} key function,
so a decoder built on it cannot be induced to lie by a badly behaved hash.

\begin{definition}[Binary-search decoder]
Run $\bsearch(\mathrm{key},t,0,n)$. If it returns $\bot$, abstain. If it returns
$m$, evaluate $\mathrm{key}(m-1)$ and $\mathrm{key}(m+1)$ where they exist; if
either equals $t$, abstain; otherwise return $a(m)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness from neighbours]
On a range with monotone key function, an index whose key differs from both
neighbours' is the unique index in $[0,n)$ with that key value.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Monotone key values are constant on blocks of consecutive indices, so a repeated
value is repeated at a neighbour of any of its occurrences.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No silent corruption, unconditionally]
The binary-search decoder never returns a wrong symbol, whatever the key function
and whatever the array; the only ingredient is Theorem~\ref{thm:bssound}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Total cost]\label{thm:total}
The binary-search decoder performs at most $\log_2 n + 3$ key evaluations.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-linear Monte-Carlo compression]\label{thm:sublinear}
Let $H$ be $2$-universal with $K\ge1$, $M\ge1$, and $l$ a codebook with typicality
parameter $\delta$. There is a key $k$ such that the scheme with sorted codebook
and binary-search decoder satisfies
\[
\Pr[\text{failure}] \le \delta + \frac{2\abs{l}}{M}, \qquad
\Pr[\text{silent corruption}] \le \frac{2\delta\abs{l}}{M},
\]
and decodes in at most $\log_2\abs{l} + 3$ key evaluations.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $k$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}. On a collision-free codebook symbol,
monotonicity and Theorem~\ref{thm:bsfound} give a hit and the neighbour test
passes; on a colliding codebook symbol the neighbour test fails and the decoder
abstains. Soundness gives the silent bound and Theorem~\ref{thm:total} the cost.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$\log_2 n + 3 < n$ for every $n \ge 6$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection*{The decision-tree converse}

\begin{definition}[Decision tree]
A decision tree over a query set $Q$ and output set $\iota$ is a finite binary
tree with internal nodes labelled by queries and leaves by outputs. An
\emph{input} is an oracle $Q\to\mathrm{Bool}$; running follows the branch given by
the answers, and $\runCost$ counts the queries asked.
\end{definition}

This subsumes every adaptive algorithm learning about its input only through
Boolean tests.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:outputs}
The set of outputs a tree can produce using at most $c$ queries has at most $2^c$
elements.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Cost converse]\label{thm:dtconv}
Let $t$ be a decision tree, $S$ a set of $n$ distinct symbols, and $\mathrm{input}$
an assignment of oracles. If $t$ outputs $x$ correctly on $\mathrm{input}(x)$ for
every $x\in S$ and $\runCost(\mathrm{input}(x))\le c$ for every $x\in S$, then
$n \le 2^c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Correctness embeds $S$ into the outputs reachable within $c$ queries; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:outputs}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For any correct decoder and nonempty $S$, some input costs at least
$\log_2\abs{S}$ queries.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Optimality within an additive $3$]
For every correct decision-tree decoder $t$ on a nonempty symbol set $S$, some
$x\in S$ satisfies
$\mathrm{cost}(\text{binary-search decoder on }\abs{S}\text{ symbols}) \le
\runCost_t(\mathrm{input}(x)) + 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Block converse]
Any scheme decoding $b$ blocks by $b$ independent coordinatewise decoders, each
correct on $n$ symbols, costs at least $b\log_2 n$ queries in total; the
coordinatewise binary-search scheme achieves $b(\log_2 n + 3)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{List decoding}\label{sec:list}

\begin{definition}[List scheme]
$\dec : \Code \to \mathrm{List}(\alpha)$ with all lists of length $\le T$; it
succeeds at $x$ if $x \in \dec(\enc(x))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[List converse]
For lists of length at most $T$,
$\Pr[\text{success}] \le T\abs{\Code}\pmaxx(\mu)$, hence
$\log\abs{\Code} + \log T \ge \Hinf(\mu) + \log(1-\varepsilon)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each codeword covers at most $T$ symbols of the success set, so
$\abs{\Succ} \le T\abs{\Code}$; then argue as in Theorem~\ref{thm:count}.
\end{proof}

At $T=1$ this is Theorem~\ref{thm:conv}: a list of length $T$ relaxes the counting
bound by exactly $\log T$ bits, and no more.

\begin{theorem}[List achievability, $2$-universal]
There is a key with $\Pr[\text{failure}] \le \delta + \abs{l}/(TM)$, lists of
length at most $T$, decoding cost exactly $\abs{l}$, and the guarantee that a
non-empty answer contains the true symbol whenever that symbol is in the codebook.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[$T$-wise independence, counting form]
$H$ satisfies $\mathrm{Indep}_T$ if for every set of $T$ symbols and every
distinct $x$, at most a fraction $M^{-T}$ of keys make all of them collide with
$x$. $\mathrm{Indep}_1$ is exactly $2$-universality.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Factorial-moment identity]
With $c_k = \abs{\Coll_k(x)}$,
$\displaystyle M^T \sum_{k\in\Keys}\binom{c_k}{T} \le K\binom{\abs{l}}{T}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Double count pairs (key, $T$-subset of the codebook all of whose members collide
with $x$ under that key); each of the $\binom{\abs{l}}{T}$ subsets admits at most
$K/M^T$ keys.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential list gain]
Under $\mathrm{Indep}_T$ there is a key with
\[
\Pr[\text{failure}] \;\le\; \delta + \binom{\abs{l}}{T}\Big/M^T \;\le\;
\delta + \Bigl(\frac{\abs{l}}{M}\Bigr)^{T},
\]
lists of length at most $T$ and decoding cost exactly $\abs{l}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Polynomial family]
Over $\mathbb{F}_p$, with key $c=(c_0,\dots,c_T)\in\mathbb{F}_p^{T+1}$, put
$h_c(x) = c_0 + c_1x + \cdots + c_Tx^T \pmod p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Interpolation]
Two coefficient vectors in $\mathbb{F}_p^{T+1}$ agreeing at $T{+}1$ distinct
points are equal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The evaluation map at $T{+}1$ distinct points is a Vandermonde matrix, whose
determinant is nonzero exactly when the points are distinct.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Short-key higher independence]
The degree-$T$ polynomial family satisfies $\mathrm{Indep}_T$ with only
$K = p^{T+1}$ keys, i.e.\ $(T{+}1)\log_2 p$ bits of advice.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Numeric instance]
Over $\mathbb{F}_{101}$ with a $10$-element codebook carrying all but $1/100$ of
the mass and $T=3$: some key in $\mathbb{F}_{101}^4$ (about $10^8$ keys, i.e.\
$27$ bits of advice, versus $101^{101}$ for the family of all functions) gives
failure probability at most $\tfrac{1}{100}+\tfrac{1}{1000}$ with lists of length
at most $3$.
\end{theorem}

\section{How much randomness does the encoder need?}\label{sec:keys}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic lower bound]
A $2$-universal family with $M\ge 2$, at least one key, on a domain of size $n$
satisfies $n \le M^K$; equivalently $K \ge \log_M n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $x \mapsto (H_k(x))_{k\in\Keys}$ is injective, since two symbols agreeing
on all keys would collide for all $K$ keys, contradicting $K\cdot M \le K$ when
$M\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp bound]
A nonempty $2$-universal family that compresses at all ($M < n$) has $K \ge M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $x\ne y$ the number of colliding keys is a natural number $\le K/M$; if $K<M$
it is $0$, so every $H_k$ is injective on a domain of size $n > M$: impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$K \ge \max(M,\log_M n)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial key space]
If $n \le M^c$ then $n \le K^c$; hence the key space is polynomially large in the
source and no $2$-universal family in that regime has $\mathrm{poly}(\log n)$ keys.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Attained]
The inner-product family of Definition~\ref{def:linhash} has exactly $K = M = p$
on a source of $n = p^2$ symbols.
\end{theorem}

The encoder's advice must be at least as long as the codeword it produces, and one
codeword's worth of advice suffices.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Encode.} Input key $k$, codebook $l$, symbol $x$; output $H_k(x)$; cost
one key evaluation.

\paragraph{Linear unique-match decode.} Scan $l$ evaluating $H_k$ on each entry,
counting matches and remembering the last; return the match iff the count is $1$,
else $\bot$. Cost exactly $\abs{l}$; never returns a wrong codebook symbol.

\paragraph{Sorted binary-search decode.} Once per key, sort $l$ by $H_k$. Per
query, binary search for the received value; if found at $m$, compare with the
keys at $m\pm1$; return $a(m)$ if both differ, else $\bot$. Cost at most
$\log_2\abs{l}+3$; never returns a wrong symbol under any hypothesis on $H_k$.
Sorting is amortized over all queries with the same key and, being a permutation,
changes no probability in the analysis.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the relaxation does and does not buy}

Theorems~\ref{thm:conv} and~\ref{thm:tight} together give the cleanest statement
of the first message: relaxing exact decoding to $(1-\varepsilon)$-decoding moves
the counting bound by exactly $\log(1-\varepsilon)$ bits, and the relaxed bound is
achieved. On a uniform source that is a negligible gain: \emph{randomness does not
buy rate}. What it buys is implementability. Under the exact requirement the
encoder must be injective, so the code is essentially an enumeration of the
alphabet; under the relaxed requirement the code may be a hash---an object with a
one-line description, a one-field-element key, and a decoder that is a binary
search.

\subsection{Two-sidedness}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
Axis & Achievability & Converse & Gap \\
\midrule
Rate & failure $\delta + \abs{l}/M$ & $\log\abs{\Code}\ge\Hinf+\log(1-\varepsilon)$ & constant \\
Lists & failure $\delta + (\abs{l}/M)^T$ & $\log\abs{\Code}+\log T\ge\Hinf+\log(1-\varepsilon)$ & exactly $\log T$ \\
Time & $\log_2 n + 3$ & $\ge\log_2 n$, any adaptive decoder & additive $3$ \\
Randomness & $K=M=p$ realized & $K\ge\max(M,\log_M n)$ & exact \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{No silent corruption}

Three independent mechanisms enforce the discipline that failures are announced:
the unique-match rule (unconditional on codebook symbols), the neighbour
uniqueness test (unconditional, for the binary-search decoder), and a checksum
from a second universal family reducing off-codebook silent corruption below any
target at $\log C$ extra bits---with the tunable threshold argument already
reducing it to $(1+\eta)\delta\abs{l}/M$ for free.

\subsection{Why min-entropy}

Every converse here is a counting statement, and counting converts to probability
through $\mu(S)\le\abs{S}\pmaxx$. For very non-flat sources this is conservative,
but for one-shot statements about a decoder that either does or does not get a
particular symbol right, min-entropy is the honest parameter, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:tight} shows it cannot be replaced by anything smaller here.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Average-case converse.} The decision-tree converse bounds worst-case
cost by $\log_2 n$. Is there a matching average-case converse
$\mathbb{E}[\runCost] \ge H(\mu)$, achieved by a Huffman-shaped rather than
balanced split of the sorted codebook?
\item \textbf{Closing the additive $3$.} Can the gap between $\log_2 n$ and
$\log_2 n+3$ be removed by folding the neighbour test into the search---searching
for the boundary of the equal-key block rather than for any member of it?
\item \textbf{Multi-region thresholds.} The tunable derandomization uses two
regions with $c_1^{-1}+c_2^{-1}\le1$; for $r$ regions the constraint is
$\sum_i c_i^{-1}\le1$. Which splits give the best simultaneous
failure/silent/list guarantees, and is the achievable region exactly the one that
constraint cuts out?
\item \textbf{Key length for higher independence.} $K \ge M$ is sharp for
$2$-universality. What is sharp for $\mathrm{Indep}_T$? The polynomial family gives
$K=p^{T+1}$ with $M=p$; is $K\ge M^T$ or $K\ge M^{T+1}$ forced?
\item \textbf{Sorting meets blocks.} Coordinatewise decoding with sorted per-block
codebooks costs $b(\log_2\abs{T}+3)$ against the converse $b\log_2\abs{T}$. Can a
joint decoder over the product codebook beat $b\log_2\abs{T}$ when the source is
not i.i.d.?
\item \textbf{Checksums versus lists.} Both $\log C$ checksum bits and $\log T$
list bits buy reliability. Is there a single ``detection--ambiguity'' budget of
which both are special cases, with a common converse?
\item \textbf{Non-uniform codebooks.} Weighting the codebook by $\mu$ and
thresholding the weighted collision mass should give bounds in terms of R\'enyi
entropies interpolating between $\Hinf$ and $H$; the first-moment machinery
appears to carry over verbatim.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Almost-lossless compression is usually presented as a story about rate. The
results here suggest a different emphasis: on the rate axis the relaxation is
worth exactly $\log(1-\varepsilon)$ bits, and the bound it relaxes to is attained,
so as a rate technique Monte-Carlo compression is nearly a no-op. Its real content
is algorithmic. The relaxation permits the code to be a keyed hash instead of an
enumeration; the observation that the codebook may be sorted \emph{after} the key
is fixed turns the decoder from an exhaustive scan into a binary search; and a
decision-tree counting argument shows the resulting cost, $\log_2 n + 3$ key
evaluations, is within an additive constant of the best any adaptive algorithm can
do. Three independent mechanisms guarantee that failures announce themselves
rather than corrupting data silently, and pigeonhole applied to the key space
shows the encoder's randomness must be at least one codeword long---and that one
codeword's worth suffices.

